# Chroma content pack, The Five Callings: THE HEALING ROAD (moments 1 to 9 of callings/MOMENTS-BRIEF.md, "The healing
# road"), Green's single-colour calling and a tendency, never a wall: a [nurse] or [midwife], then [senior nurse or
# midwife] (the Known rung), then [renowned healer] (the summit, a facet on the healing career held). Pathways and packs
# thread, 2026-10-19, for v24; switched off until `callings` is in batch.PACKS. Syntax: header of chroma-library/build.py.
# Rules: chroma-library/SPEC.md, notes/library-spec.md and callings/MOMENTS-BRIEF.md (pack rules, calibration lessons,
# balance layout). Names in title:, grants:, takes_if_fails:, holds:, requires: and aims: come from callings/catalogue.py,
# core/*.py and the base catalogue (chroma-library/earth_perks_titles.py: nurse, midwife, care worker, paramedic,
# physician, complementary therapist, emergency care, used to night shifts, organising people, calming people down,
# professional registration, apprentice of one's own); each title and perk takes its tribal and magic form from
# callings/worlds.py (the band's birth-keeper and first healer, the healer every band sends for; a healer of the temple
# house, a herb-healer known across the realm).
#
# The road leans on life, body, birth and growth (the Vision thread, 10-19), so it never reads as White's duty and
# service: a baby caught on a farmhouse floor, a ward kept through a short night, a long life ending quietly among its
# own, the town's birth centre, a dressing from the garden, a fever nursed through a winter camp. Each colour's honest
# road runs through every moment: White's protocol, Blue's diagnosis and method, Black's leverage and name, Red's night
# nobody else will work, Green's body, birth, garden and patience. Real and unglamorous where it is: night shifts, short
# staff, paperwork, managers' reviews; a step up is a good year, not a finish line.
#
# Nine moments. Two holder pairs (everyday, holds:, rate .5, gap 1-3, no share: line: the holds: gate does the drawing),
# whose halves share stages, ages, holds:, rate and gap, split the colours 2 and 3 and use the cycle on the pair's header
# line; they take no new title (calibration lesson 1): the next rung is an aims:, and grants: gives only a perk plainly
# earned. Then four life events and the tribal world's own event, each even on its own (alpha even, stakes 1.0, the five
# one-colour options and the combination half named in its header comment, every colour's mean the same inside the
# moment): the two steps at their real chances (the ward 40, the summit 6, the same for every colour, with the
# earned-odds lifts of helps.py on top in the engine; no perk on failure), the fall, the long shot (3, with
# grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed) and the winter camp. Every option of a life event leaves one or two
# footprints (chroma-library/drafts/footprints-brief.md): the fields it already has, or a foot: just before chance:.
#
# Content limits, never broken: no baby or child dies or is harmed in any text, and the one birth ends with mother and
# baby well in every outcome; no option helps anyone die or hastens a death, and the one death (an old patient at the end
# of a long illness) is natural, peaceful and plain; illness and injury stay plain and offstage; suicide is never pickable
# and never a scene; no sexual violence. Children appear only in a family, with a parent present. No real person,
# hospital, medicine, brand, place, country or year is named: "the big unit in the city", "a strong painkiller", "the
# national nursing college". Modern Earth has no magic, and the magic world's healers here work with herbs, hands and
# the temple house. In the tribal world the one supernatural act is a spirit's cure asked through the shaman (moment 9):
# one choice among ordinary ones, priced on a closed: approval: line with a backfire, never the person's own gift.
# Wrongs and closed acts are pickable with plain labels, each marked, with a real backfire, spread over the colours:
# checks signed that were not done (W), a managers' review leaked to the paper (B), a post refused on conditions (UR), a
# dose mistake hidden (UBG), a dressing used on the ward without leave (URG), the spirit's price promised (WRG).
# Every moment carries earth:, tribal: and magic: lines (moment 9 the tribal one alone) and an untagged scene for each
# other world; an option whose act reads differently there carries its own tribal: or magic: wording; works and fails
# lines are written to read in every world.

# ================================================================ holder pairs

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- nurse, midwife, care worker or paramedic: calls RG / calls WUB (cycle s2)

== the birth that will not wait
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
gap: 1-3
times: per nurse, midwife, care worker or paramedic a year: about 1 in 30 is there when a baby comes too fast to reach the hospital or the midwife, paramedics and midwives most often (estimate); about 1 birth in 200 comes before the mother reaches help (estimate); about 3 lives in 100 are nurses, 8 in 100 care workers, 4 in 1,000 paramedics and 1 in 1,000 midwives (catalogue shares)
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-75
calls: RG
holds: nurse | midwife | care worker | paramedic
tone: joy
life: work, family
horizon: moment
roles: colleague, mentor
earth: a farmhouse at the top of a lane the snowplough has not reached, and a baby coming two weeks early, an hour before any ambulance can
tribal: the band moving camp through the first snow, and a young mother's pains coming fast, a day's walk from the birth shelter
magic: a coaching inn cut off by snow on the high road, and the innkeeper's daughter's baby coming early, a day's ride from the temple house
scene: {N} is on the last home visit of the day, at a farm at the top of a lane the snowplough has not reached, when the old farmer's granddaughter, staying for the winter, goes into labour two weeks early. The pains are already close together. The ambulance says an hour at best, and the only thing in the kitchen that is ready is the kettle.
scene W: The emergency line has a script for this, and {N} has done the course. Clean towels, gloves, the time written down: there is an order to things, even on a kitchen floor.
scene U: {N} counts the minutes between the pains and watches the young woman's face. The numbers say this baby will not wait for any ambulance.
scene B: Somebody has to run this kitchen for the next hour. The farmer is shaking, the young woman's partner is on the phone to his mother, and {N} is the only one in the room who has seen a birth.
scene R: {Ns} heart is going like a drum, and that is fine. The baby is coming, {N} is here, and nothing else matters now.
scene G: Babies have been born in farm kitchens for as long as there have been farms. The stove is warm, the snow is quiet, and her body knows what it is doing.
scene tribal: The band is moving camp along the river in the first snow when a young mother sits down hard on the path, her pains coming fast. The birth shelter is a day's walk behind them, and {N}, who has helped at many births, kneels beside her while the others pile up wood for a fire.
scene magic: Snow has closed the high road, and the coaching inn is full of stranded travellers when the innkeeper's daughter's waters break, a fortnight early. The temple house is a day's ride away, and {N}, a nurse of its wards on the way home, is the only healer under the roof.
- W | ring the emergency line, and do each thing the call handler says, in order | v: conformity, security | tribal: send a runner back for the old birth-keeper, and do each thing as she taught, in order | magic: send the stable boy riding for the temple house, and do each thing as the old midwives teach | chance: 80
- U | time the pains, and feel how the baby lies, before deciding anything | v: achievement, security | chance: 75
- B | take charge of the kitchen, and give everyone in the house a job | v: power, security | aims: senior nurse or midwife | tribal: take charge of the path, and give everyone in the band a job | magic: take charge of the inn's kitchen, and give every stranded traveller a job | chance: 75
- R | kneel on the kitchen floor, and catch the baby there and then | v: stimulation, benevolence | body: light | grants: emergency care | tribal: kneel in the snow by the new fire, and catch the baby there and then | magic: kneel on the inn's hearth, and catch the baby there and then | chance: 70
- G | keep her warm by the stove, and let the birth come at its own pace | v: benevolence, tradition | self_control: + | grants: families who send for you | tribal: keep her warm by the fire under the furs, and let the birth come in its own time | magic: keep her warm by the inn's hearth, and let the birth come at its own pace | chance: 75
- W>B | write the birth up for the hospital that night, with your name and every time | v: achievement, conformity | aims: senior nurse or midwife | tribal: tell the birth at the fire that night, step by step, so the elders know whose hands did it | magic: write the birth up for the temple house that night, with your name and every hour | chance: 60
- U>R | judge from the pains that there is still time, and drive her down the lane | v: stimulation, self-direction | tribal: judge from the pains that there is still time, and walk her on to the birth shelter | magic: judge from the pains that there is still time, and drive her down to the village in the sleigh | chance: 65
- B>G | phone the farmer down the valley, and talk him into clearing the lane with his tractor | v: benevolence, power | mark: made a friend | tribal: send word to the next band's hunters, and talk them into breaking a trail through the snow | magic: talk the stranded carters into digging the road out to the village | chance: 65
- R>W | send everyone out of the kitchen, loudly, and stand guard at the door | v: security, benevolence | tribal: send the band on ahead, loudly, and keep a quiet ring round the mother | magic: send the gawping travellers out of the kitchen, loudly, and stand guard at the door | chance: 70
- G>U | let her body lead, and note every sign for the midwives who come after | v: universalism, achievement | tribal: let her body lead, and remember every sign to tell the old birth-keeper | magic: let her body lead, and note every sign for the temple's midwives | chance: 65
works: The baby comes crying and strong, and mother and baby are warm and well long before help arrives.
works: Within the week everyone around has heard the story, and the new mother asks for {N} by name.
fails: It is a frightening hour and a messy one, but mother and baby are both well when help comes.
fails: Others take over at the last minute and {Ns} part is lost in the rush, though mother and baby are both well.

== the ward is two nurses short
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
gap: 1-3
times: per nurse, midwife, care worker or paramedic a year: about 1 in 2 work a night short of staff at least once, and many work one every month (staff surveys; estimate); about 3 lives in 100 are nurses and 8 in 100 care workers (catalogue shares)
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-75
calls: WUB
holds: nurse | midwife | care worker | paramedic
tone: trouble
life: work, health
horizon: week
roles: colleague, boss
earth: the night shift on a ward of twenty-eight beds, two nurses short again, and the agency has nobody to send
tribal: the sickness shelter in the deep of winter, the sick laid close by the fire, and only two pairs of hands to tend them through the night
magic: the long ward of the temple house at night, two sisters down with the cough, and every bed full
scene: Eight o'clock at night, and all twenty-eight beds on the ward are full. The rota says four nurses; there are two, and {colleague} qualified a month ago. The agency has nobody to send, the manager's phone goes to voicemail, and the drug round starts in half an hour.
scene W: Every patient has checks due every four hours, and a box for each on the chart. Tonight there are not enough hands to fill them all, and {N} knows what an empty box can mean.
scene U: Twenty-eight patients, two nurses, twelve hours. {N} is already working out which ones need watching every hour and which can safely sleep.
scene B: This is the third short night this month, and every one of them was signed off upstairs as fine. Someone up there is counting on nobody making a fuss.
scene R: {Ns} legs are tired already, and the night has not started. There is nothing for it but to go, and keep going.
scene G: The old man in bed six cannot settle unless someone sits with him a while, and the woman in bed nine is frightened of the dark. No chart on the ward says so.
scene tribal: Deep in the winter the sick of the band lie close by the fire in the sickness shelter: an old woman with a cough, a hunter with a hurt leg, a mother with a fever. Two of the band's healers are down with it themselves, and {N} and {colleague} are the only hands left to tend them through the long night.
scene magic: Every bed in the temple house's long ward is full, and two of the sisters are down with the cough. The night matron has gone to the city on the temple's business, and {N} and {colleague} have the whole ward between them until dawn.
- W | sign the night's checks as done, so every chart is complete by morning | v: conformity, security | mark: hid a wrong | self_control: - | closed: approval: checks signed that were not done; backfire: the audit finds the gaps, and the ward manager asks who signed them | tribal: tell the elders at dawn that every one of the sick was tended through the night | magic: sign the night book as if every round was made | chance: 80
- U | rank the patients by need, and plan the whole night hour by hour | v: achievement, security | grants: organising people | tribal: rank the sick by need, and plan the whole night by the stars | chance: 75
- B | refuse to take more admissions until the ward has the staff it needs | v: power, security | mark: defied an authority | tribal: tell the elders no more of the sick may come to the shelter until there are more hands | magic: refuse the steward any more patients until the ward has the sisters it needs | chance: 70
- R | work straight through the night with no break, and cover both bays | v: benevolence, stimulation | body: light | self_control: + | grants: used to night shifts | tribal: tend the sick straight through the night with no rest, both sides of the fire | magic: work straight through the night with no rest, and cover both ends of the ward | chance: 75
- G | sit with the frightened ones first, and let the paperwork wait | v: benevolence, universalism | self_control: - | tribal: sit with the frightened ones first, and let the rest wait their turn | magic: sit with the frightened ones first, and let the ward book wait | chance: 70
- W>B | fill in the staffing report in full, and send it to the director | v: conformity, power | aims: senior nurse or midwife | tribal: tell the elders plainly at the fire how few hands there were, and ask for more | magic: write the night's shortfall in the matron's book, and send it to the temple's steward | chance: 60
- U>R | agree a signal with the other nurse, so either can run to the other's bay | v: self-direction, stimulation | tribal: agree a call with the other healer, so either can run to the other's side of the fire | magic: agree a signal with the other sister, so either can run to the other's end of the ward | chance: 65
- B>G | call in a favour from the next ward, and borrow a nurse for the drug round | v: benevolence, power | tribal: call in a favour from the next hearth, and borrow a pair of hands for the night | magic: call in a favour from the sisters' wing, and borrow a nurse for the medicine round | chance: 70
- R>W | tell the bed manager to their face that the ward is not safe tonight | v: security, universalism | mark: defied an authority | tribal: tell the eldest to their face that the sick are not safe with so few hands | magic: tell the steward to their face that the ward is not safe tonight | chance: 65
- G>U | show the families staying late how to help with drinks and meals | v: benevolence, universalism | door | tribal: show the kin sitting by the fire how to help with water and broth | chance: 70
works: Dawn comes, every patient is safe, and the day staff find the night's work done.
works: The night is hard but it holds, and the next week someone finally finds more hands for the nights.
fails: The night holds, just, and {N} goes home grey with tiredness, knowing it will happen again before the month is out.
fails: Something small is missed in the rush, nobody is harmed, and the morning brings a hard conversation.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- senior nurse or midwife: calls WU / calls BRG (cycle s4)

== a family asks you to stay to the end
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
gap: 1-3
times: per senior nurse or midwife a year: about 1 in 4 are asked by a family to be with them at the end (estimate); about half of all deaths in rich countries come after a long illness, in a hospital, a hospice or a care home (estimate); about 4 lives in 1,000 are senior nurses or midwives (catalogue share)
stages: adult mature elder
age: 26-75
calls: WU
holds: senior nurse or midwife
tone: mixed
life: work, meaning
horizon: week
roles: colleague, mentor
earth: an old patient near the end of a long illness, and a daughter who asks the senior nurse to be there when it comes
tribal: an old hunter of the band near the end of a long sickness, and his kin asking the birth-keeper to sit with them through his last nights
magic: an old woman in the quiet room of the temple house, near the end of a long illness, and her son asking the healer to stay
scene: The old woman in the side room has been on the ward five weeks, and the end of her long illness is close now: a day, perhaps two. She sleeps most of the time and is in no pain. Her daughter finds {N} at the nurses' station, holding a cup of tea she has forgotten to drink, and asks whether {N} will be there when it comes.
scene W: There is a plan for the last days, and {N} wrote most of it: comfort, mouth care, the family's wishes, the words of their faith if they want them. A plan like that deserves to be kept well.
scene U: {N} has seen many deaths and knows the signs: the breathing that changes, the hands that cool. The family does not, and not knowing frightens them more than anything.
scene B: The bed managers want the side room for a new admission and have asked twice today. {N} has the seniority to say no, and means to use it.
scene R: {N} liked her from the first day: the jokes about the food, the stories about dancing when she was young. The ward will be quieter without her.
scene G: Eighty-nine years, and now her life is coming to its end the way lives do. {N} wants the end to be quiet, and warm, and among her own.
scene tribal: The old hunter has lain in his shelter since the first frosts, growing lighter every day, and now he sleeps more than he wakes, without pain. His sons and daughters sit round the fire outside, and the eldest daughter comes to {N}, the band's birth-keeper and first healer, and asks {N} to sit with them through the last nights.
scene magic: In the quiet room at the end of the temple house's long ward, an old woman of the town has come to the last days of a long illness, peaceful and without pain. Her family keep watch by the window, and her son asks {N}, a healer of the temple house, whether {N} will stay with them when the time comes.
- W | keep to her care plan hour by hour, and hold the night staff to it | v: conformity, benevolence | habit | tribal: tend him by the old ways hour by hour, and hold the other healers to them | magic: keep to the temple's rule for the dying hour by hour, and hold the night sisters to it | chance: 75
- U | explain to the family plainly what each change means, and what comes next | v: benevolence, universalism | grants: calming people down | tribal: tell his kin plainly what each change means, and what comes next | chance: 75
- B | use your seniority to keep the side room, whatever the bed managers say | v: power, benevolence | tribal: use your standing to keep the shelter quiet and unmoved, whatever the hunters say about breaking camp | magic: use your standing to keep the quiet room, whatever the steward says about beds | chance: 80
- R | stay on through the night in your own time, holding her hand | v: benevolence | grants: families who send for you | aims: renowned healer | tribal: stay through the night at his side, holding his hand | chance: 90
- G | dim the lights, open the window, and let the family sit close in their own way | v: tradition, benevolence | tribal: open the shelter to the night air, and let his kin sit close in their own way | magic: dim the lamps, open the window, and let the family sit close in their own way | chance: 75
- W>G | ask the family's own minister or elder in, as the family wishes | v: tradition, conformity | door | tribal: ask the keeper of the rites to come and sing the old songs over him, as his kin wish | magic: send for the family's own priest, as they wish | chance: 65
- U>W | write a plan for her last nights that the night staff can follow exactly | v: security, conformity | aims: renowned healer | tribal: teach the other healers each thing to do in his last nights, so it is done right | magic: write a plan for her last nights that the night sisters can follow exactly | chance: 65
- B>U | call in a favour, and bring the hospice's specialist nurse to the bedside tonight | v: security, achievement | door | tribal: send for the old healer of the next band, who knows best how to ease the last nights | magic: call in a favour, and bring the temple's most learned herb-healer to the bedside tonight | chance: 60
- R>B | tell her story at the managers' meeting, and win the ward a proper quiet room | v: achievement, benevolence | tribal: tell his story at the elders' fire, and win the sick a shelter of their own, away from the noise | magic: tell her story to the temple council, and win the ward a quiet room of its own | chance: 50
- G>R | help the family bring in her old dog and the songs she loved | v: hedonism, benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | tribal: help his kin bring in his old hunting dog and sing the songs he loved | magic: help the family bring in her old cat and the songs she loved | chance: 65
works: The end comes quietly in the early hours, with the family close, and nobody is alone.
works: Some weeks later the family sends word of thanks, and it names {N}.
fails: The end comes peacefully on a night {N} is not there, and the family is sorry, and grateful all the same.
fails: The last days are calm, but the family quarrels in the corridor, and {N} cannot mend it.

== the managers want the birth centre closed
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
gap: 1-3
times: per senior nurse or midwife a year: about 1 in 15 see their own unit, a small birth centre or a ward, put up for closing or merging (estimate); small midwife-led birth centres open and close with each round of budgets (estimate); about 4 lives in 1,000 are senior nurses or midwives (catalogue share)
stages: adult mature elder
age: 26-75
calls: BRG
holds: senior nurse or midwife
tone: trouble
life: work, community
horizon: months
roles: boss, colleague
earth: the hospital's managers plan to close the small birth centre in town and send every birth to the big unit in the city, forty minutes away
tribal: the elders talk of giving up the birth shelter by the spring, and sending the band's mothers to the big camp's healers downriver
magic: the temple council means to shut the birthing house in the lower town, and send every mother up the hill to the great temple
scene: The letter comes on a Monday: after a review, the managers propose to close the town's small birth centre and send every birth to the big unit in the city, forty minutes away by road. {boss} calls it a consultation. Seven hundred babies were born in the centre last year, and half the town's families know {N} by name.
scene W: There is a proper process for this: a consultation, a safety review, a vote of the board. {N} means to answer every page of it.
scene U: {N} has read the review's figures, and they leave things out: the forty-minute drive in winter, the mothers whose babies come fast.
scene B: The managers want a saving they can show the board. {N} knows the councillors, the editor of the local paper and two of the board by name.
scene R: {N} reads the letter twice, and then has to go and stand outside in the car park for a while.
scene G: Three generations of the town were born in that building. Families who never set foot in the city bring their babies back to show the midwives.
scene tribal: The elders talk at the fire of giving up the birth shelter in the willows by the spring, where the band's mothers have had their babies for longer than anyone remembers, and sending the mothers to the big camp's healers downriver instead. {boss}, the eldest, says it would be one shelter fewer to keep.
scene magic: The temple council writes that the old birthing house in the lower town costs too much in silver and lamp oil, and that from midsummer every mother of the town must climb the hill to the great temple to give birth. {boss}, the council's steward, calls it a kindness.
- W | answer the consultation point by point, through every proper channel | v: conformity, security | tribal: speak at the elders' fire in your turn, as the custom is, for keeping the shelter | magic: answer the council's letter point by point, through every proper channel | chance: 70
- U | work out what the long drive would mean in winter, and publish the figures | v: universalism, achievement | identity | tribal: count the days' walk to the big camp in deep snow, and lay it out for the elders | magic: reckon the climb up the hill in winter for every mother, and set it before the council | chance: 75
- B | leak the managers' review to the local paper before the consultation opens | v: power | mark: defied an authority | closed: approval: a confidential review leaked; backfire: the managers trace the leak, and a written warning goes on the senior nurse's record | tribal: tell the other bands' elders what this band's elders plan, before they can decide it | magic: pass the council's letter to the broadsheet printers before the council can meet | chance: 75
- R | stand up at the public meeting, and say unscripted what the centre means | v: self-direction, universalism | identity | tribal: stand up at the band's fire, and say from the heart what the shelter means | magic: stand up at the town's open meeting, and say from the heart what the birthing house means | chance: 80
- G | ask the families born there to come and tell the managers their own stories | v: tradition, benevolence | grants: organising people | tribal: ask the families born in the shelter to come to the fire and tell their own stories | magic: ask the families born there to come before the council and tell their own stories | chance: 75
- W>G | draw up a plan for a smaller centre run by the midwives, within every rule | v: security, tradition | tribal: ask the elders for a smaller shelter by the spring, kept by the old women in the proper way | magic: draw up a plan for a smaller birthing house run by the midwives, within the temple's rules | chance: 70
- U>W | redesign the centre's rota so it costs less and meets every standard | v: security, achievement | tribal: work out how the shelter can be kept with fewer hands and every custom still kept | magic: redesign the birthing house's watches so they cost less and keep every rule | chance: 65
- B>U | strike a deal with the university to run the centre as a teaching unit | v: achievement, power | binds | tribal: strike a bargain with the big camp's healers to send their young ones to learn at the shelter | magic: strike a bargain with the apothecaries' college to train its students at the birthing house | chance: 65
- R>B | open a birth centre of your own, with the midwives who would lose theirs | v: self-direction, achievement | requires: savings | without: means | lacking: .5 | grants: a clinic of your own | binds | aims: renowned healer | tribal: build a healer's lodge of your own by the spring, with the old women who would lose the shelter | magic: open a house of healing of your own in the lower town, with the midwives who would lose theirs | chance: 60
- G>R | start a home-birth service for the town, run from your own car | v: self-direction, tradition | tribal: go out to the mothers in their own shelters, wherever the band makes camp | magic: go out to the mothers in their own houses, with your bag and your lamp | chance: 65
works: The managers put the closing off for a year, and the centre's lights stay on.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the mothers of the town still have somewhere near home to give birth.
fails: The centre closes by the summer, and the mothers of the town must go further to give birth.
fails: The fight drags on for months and wears everyone thin, and nothing is settled.

# ================================================================ steps

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5. the ward needs someone in charge (pairs D5a)
# The Known rung: the ward's manager retires and the post is open, for a nurse or midwife of four years or more (tenure,
# and LIFE_CALLINGS in engine-conditions.py). Every colour's way of taking it on is a try at the brief's real chance for
# the ward or birth centre to run (40): `title: senior nurse or midwife`. White's formal application and Blue's course
# rest on [professional registration]; someone without it is taken on only by the managers' leave (`requires:
# professional registration | without: approval | lacking: .3`). The pairs at 82 are the honest other ways: act up for
# six months first, a specialist's post with better hours, the post refused until the ward is staffed (a closed:
# approval act), a colleague's name put forward, and the team asked who they want. Every colour's mean is 61.

== the ward needs someone in charge
tier: life event
stages: adult mature
age: 26-67
holds: nurse | midwife
tenure: 4-100
per_year: 0 0 0 .06 .04 0
drivers: prosper+.1 community+.2
times: per nurse or midwife of four years or more a year: about 1 in 20 see the post of ward manager, charge nurse or lead midwife open where they work, and weigh going for it (estimate); about 1 registered nurse or midwife in 8 reaches that grade (workforce statistics), about 4 lives in 1,000 (catalogue share)
likelier: a manager retiring, a new ward or birth centre opening, good years with money for posts, a team that wants one of its own
rarer: a freeze on posts, a manager who stays for twenty years, a hospital closing beds
gap: 2-5
domain: career.5
tone: mixed
life: work
horizon: months
roles: mentor, colleague, rival, boss
earth: the ward manager retires, and the post of the one in charge of the ward is advertised
tribal: the old birth-keeper of the band can no longer walk to the births, and the women ask who will keep the births and the sick after her
magic: the old matron of the temple house's long ward goes to her rest, and the council asks who will keep the ward after her
scene: {mentor}, who has run the ward for nineteen years, retires at the end of the year, and the post is on the noticeboard: ward manager, interviews in a month. {rival} from the next ward has already asked for the forms. Half the team have told {N} to go for it, and the other half are waiting to see who does.
scene W: The post has a description, a list of standards and a panel. {N} reads every line, and knows the standards by heart.
scene U: {N} has had ideas about the ward for years: the handover that takes too long, the drug round that could be safer. In charge, they could be tried.
scene B: Ward manager is the first rung that counts. {N} wants it before {rival} gets it, and knows exactly who sits on the panel.
scene R: {N} has never wanted a desk. But the ward needs someone who will fight for it, and {N} can feel that it should be {N}.
scene G: {N} knows every family that comes back to the ward, and every creak of the old building. Nobody else has been here so long, through so many winters of it.
scene tribal: The old birth-keeper of the band has grown too stiff to walk to the births, and the women have begun to ask who will keep the births and tend the sick after her. {mentor}, the old birth-keeper herself, has looked at {N} more than once, and {rival} from the other hearth has been gathering plants and saying so.
scene magic: The old matron of the temple house's long ward has gone to her rest after thirty years, and the temple council will name someone to keep the ward at the new moon. {rival}, from the sisters' wing, has already been to see the steward.
